  "date": "Wed, 12 Aug 2026 21:29:26 -0400",
  "body_text": "96 \r\n🚉 Plus: Grand Central moment | Wednesday, August 12, 2026\r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n View in browser \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n Axios Finish Line By\r\nMike Allen and Erica Pandey and Jim VandeHei\r\n &middot; Aug 12, 2026 \r\nAug 12, 2026\r\n \r\n \r\n Welcome back! Smart Brevity™ count: 429 words … 1½ mins. Edited by Amy Stern. \n\n\r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n1 big thing: Drop your defenses\r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n Illustration: Brendan Lynch/Axios. Stock: Getty Images\n\n\r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n Ever heard the phrase \"hat in hand\"? Ever tried to embody it?\n\n 🎩 It's perhaps the least natural way for me to operate in the world. But I've been trying it more, Axios' Natalie Daher writes. Why it matters: For all sorts of reasons, we can default to defensiveness when a partner, friend or co-worker tells us we're doing something wrong. That pesky habit can end a conversation that's supposed to clear the air.\n\n TIME's Angela Haupt recently put together a useful guide to getting less defensive. 🖼️ The big picture: Defensiveness often feels more like setting the record straight. But it can make us more entrenched in our own perspective and less able to hear someone else's.\n\n \"The more defensive we become, the lonelier we become,\" psychiatrist Jeff Katzman tells TIME. 🏳️ A few tactics for waving the white flag:\n\n 1. ❤️‍🔥 Notice your physical warning signs. Maybe your chest tightens or your pulse spikes.\n\n That can tell you to slow down and take a beat before responding. 2. 🪞 Figure out what feels threatening. Often, it's not the point you're arguing about. It's your sense of yourself: that you're thoughtful, caring or good at your job.\n\n That can make a simple request seem like a personal attack. 3. 🫱 Trade certainty for curiosity. One of my favorite parts of aging is the creeping acceptance that I'm not nearly as certain as I once thought. In fact, I know very little at all.\n\n I took improv for two weeks and quit out of shame and mortification. But one tenet survives: \"Yes, and.\" The idea applies here: Acknowledge the other person's experience. Hearing somebody out doesn't require surrendering your own viewpoint. 4. 🧮 Find the 2% truth. New York clinical psychologist Eli Kraiem, who specializes in couples therapy, uses this frame: Even if you think 98% of a critique is bunk, identify and own a small piece of it.\n\n 5. 😈 \"Always\" and \"never\" are devil words. \"But\" isn't your friend either. They invite generalizations, and can incite the other party.\n\n 6. 🧰 Keep a repair line handy. \n\n Try: \"Help me understand what I'm missing here.\" Or go for the apology: \"I'm sorry. Can we try that again?\" The bottom line: Hat in hand doesn't mean admitting you're wrong about everything. It means dropping the impulse to win long enough to hear what the other person is trying to tell you.\n\n\r\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n &nbsp; \r\nA message from Axios\r\n\n\r\n \r\n Don't Miss: Our Quarterly Report on Ad Economics \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n Media Trends Executive just released its exclusive quarterly report on the forces reshaping ad economics.\n\n\r\n \r\n Built for executives, it pairs expert reporting with essential data and charts to show where ad dollars are moving next. \r\n \r\n 🔒 Become a member today to unlock this exclusive Media Trends Executive report.\n\n\r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n2. 🏛️ Parting shot: New York minute\r\n \r\n \r\n Photo: Timothy A. Clary/AFP via Getty Images A traveler walks past Grand Central Terminal's towering arched windows in Manhattan earlier this week.\n\n\r\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n 🏁 Please invite your friends to join Finish Line . \n\n\r\n \r\n 👆 Like this comms style and format? It's called Smart Brevity®. Bring it to your org — via hands-on training or internal comms software — to harness its power and impact. \r\n \r\n &nbsp; Axios thanks our partners for supporting our journalism.\n Advertise with us. \n\n Read more about Axios' mission , ethics and how we use AI in our journalism . You can reach the authors by replying to this email.\nSponsorship has no influence on editorial content.\n\n Thank you for signing up for this Axios newsletter. To stop receiving this newsletter, unsubscribe or manage your email preferences . If someone forwarded this to you, sign up now to get Axios in your inbox.\n\n Axios, PO Box 101060, Arlington VA 22201 &nbsp; Follow Axios across:\n\n &nbsp; &nbsp; \r\n \r\n&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;",
