tr><tr><td style=\"padding: 0 3.5%; line-height: 1.3;\"><span class=\"b\" style=\"font-size: 13px; color: #848484;\">\r\n<p>Illustration: Brendan Lynch/Axios. Stock: Getty Images</p>\r\n</span></td></tr>\r\n<tr><td height=\"15\" style=\"height: 15px; font-size: 0;\">&nbsp;</td></tr>\r\n<tr><td class=\"v\" style=\"padding: 0 3.5%; line-height: 1.75; font-size: 17px; font-family: Georgia, serif; line-height: 1.75; color: #222222; letter-spacing: -0.1px;\">\r\n<p><strong>Ever heard the phrase</strong> \"hat in hand\"? Ever tried to embody it?</p><ul><li>🎩 <strong>It's perhaps</strong> the least natural way for me to operate in the world. But I've been trying it more, <em>Axios' Natalie Daher writes.</em></li></ul><p><strong>Why it matters:</strong> For all sorts of reasons, we can default to defensiveness when a partner, friend or co-worker tells us we're doing something wrong. That pesky habit can end a conversation that's supposed to clear the air.</p><ul><li><strong>TIME's Angela Haupt</strong> recently put together a <a href=\"https://link.axios.com/click/47014948.427696/aHR0cHM6Ly90aW1lLmNvbS9hcnRpY2xlLzIwMjYvMDcvMjkvaG93LXRvLXN0b3AtYmVpbmctZGVmZW5zaXZlLWluLWFyZ3VtZW50cy8_dXRtX3NvdXJjZT1uZXdzbGV0dGVyJnV0bV9tZWRpdW09ZW1haWwmdXRtX2NhbXBhaWduPW5ld3NsZXR0ZXJfYXhpb3NmaW5pc2hsaW5lJnN0cmVhbT10b3A/6a14a774a4e0095e0a03c94aB29e2aced\" target=\"_blank\">useful guide</a> to getting less defensive. </li></ul><p><strong>🖼️ The big picture:</strong> Defensiveness often <em>feels</em> more like setting the record straight. But it can make us more entrenched in our own perspective and less able to hear someone else's.</p><ul><li><strong>\"The more defensive</strong> we become, the lonelier we become,\" psychiatrist Jeff Katzman tells TIME.</li></ul><p>🏳️ <strong>A few tactics</strong> for waving the white flag:</p><p><strong>1.</strong> ❤️‍🔥 <strong>Notice your physical warning signs.</strong> Maybe your chest tightens or your pulse spikes.</p><ul><li><strong>That can tell you</strong> to slow down and take a beat before responding.</li></ul><p><strong>2. </strong>🪞 <strong>Figure out what feels threatening.</strong> Often, it's not the point you're arguing about. It's your sense of yourself: that you're thoughtful, caring or good at your job.</p><ul><li><strong>That can make</strong> a simple request seem like a personal attack.</li></ul><p><strong>3. </strong>🫱 <strong>Trade certainty for curiosity. </strong>One of my favorite parts of aging is the creeping acceptance that I'm not nearly as certain as I once thought. In fact, I know very little at all.</p><ul><li><strong>I took improv</strong> for two weeks and quit out of shame and mortification. But one tenet survives: \"Yes, and.\"</li><li><strong>The idea applies here:</strong> Acknowledge the other person's experience. Hearing somebody out doesn't require surrendering your own viewpoint.</li></ul><p><strong>4. 🧮 Find the 2% truth.</strong> New York clinical psychologist Eli Kraiem, who specializes in couples therapy, uses this frame: Even if you think 98% of a critique is bunk, identify and own a small piece of it.</p><p><strong>5. </strong>😈 <strong>\"Always\" and \"never\" are devil words. </strong>\"But\" isn't your friend either. They invite generalizations, and can incite the other party.</p><p><strong>6. </strong>🧰 <strong>Keep a repair line handy.</strong> </p><ul><li><strong>Try:</strong> \"Help me understand what I'm missing here.\" </li><li><strong>Or go for the apology:</strong> \"I'm sorry. Can we try that again?\"</li></ul><p><strong>The bottom line:</strong> Hat in hand doesn't mean admitting you're wrong about everything. It means dropping the impulse to win long enough to hear what the other person is trying to tell you.</p>\r\n</td></tr>\r\n<tr><td align=\"center\" style=\"padding:20px 3.5% 25px\">   <a href=\"https://link.axios.com/click/47014948.427696/aHR0cHM6Ly93d3cuZmFjZWJvb2suY29tL3NoYXJlci5waHA_dT1odHRwczovL3d3dy5heGlvcy5jb20vbmV3c2xldHRlcnMvYXhpb3MtZmluaXNoLWx